cartridge "RINGSIDE KARATE/COMPUTE GAZETTE/1986"

' RINGSIDE KARATE
' A ColecoVision game based on "Ringside Karate" by Anthony Bertram,
' COMPUTE!'s Gazette, October 1986, p. 52 (Commodore 64, machine code). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in
' Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a byte-for-byte port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/karate.dis, timed in jsc64 with orig/c64run-karate.mjs),
' the game is rebuilt for the ColecoVision with new fighters, ring, sounds
' and a title tune.
'
' The game: two karateka in a ring, one in a white gi (left, controller 1)
' and one in a black gi (right, controller 2). Walk in and out; a button
' with the stick toward the other fighter punches, with toward-and-up
' kicks, with toward-and-down sweeps the feet; a button with any other
' direction (or none) blocks. A move that reaches the other fighter while
' he is neither attacking nor blocking knocks him down (100 points); a
' block stops a punch or a kick but not a foot sweep. Ten knockdowns
' (1000) win. The button must be let go for a moment between moves: pressed
' again too quickly, the fighter freezes and is easy to knock down.
'
' From the original (orig/karate.dis): white on the left, black on the
' right; the four moves and their stick directions (up-toward, toward,
' down-toward, anything else = block); the button must be let go between
' two moves; walking in and out with a closest distance and the ring's
' sides; a hit counts only when the attacker is in a move and the other
' fighter is in neither a move nor a block (two moves at once score
' nothing, a block cancels a hit); 100 points a hit, the hit fighter
' knocked back, then both back to their starting places; the first to 1000
' wins ("WHITE WINS!" / "BLACK WINS!"); the scores and RINGSIDE KARATE on
' the ring's apron.
'
' From the article: the moves and their directions, block "a limited
' defense", ten knockdowns of 100 points, and the button released between
' attacks ("if the button is pressed rapidly, the fighter won't move and
' will likely be knocked down"): here pressing again within 4 frames of
' letting go freezes the fighter for 24 frames.
'
' Liberties: the C64 ran one pass of about 0.19 s (a move shown for 0.13 s,
' then checked); here every move has a wind-up, a striking moment and a
' recovery in sixtieths of a second (punch quick and short, low kick
' middling, high kick slow and long), and a fighter in wind-up or recovery
' can be hit; a blocked move staggers its attacker; two moves that meet
' push both fighters apart; a block lasts a moment and is followed by a
' short recovery (on the C64 it lasted one pass too). Reach is in pixels
' between the fighters (the C64 used sprite collisions). Walking is 30
' pixels a second (the C64: 5 pixels a pass, about 26 a second). Added: a
' one-player mode against a computer black belt that reacts no faster than
' a person (it decides every 8 to 15 frames) and learns to block a move
' repeated over and over, so neither standing still nor mashing one move
' wins; a READY and the bell before every bout; a title, a credits page,
' sounds and an original title tune ("Bow In"). A 90-second match clock
' (fighting time, shown at the top): at time-out the higher score wins and
' equal scores are a DRAW (the C64 match had no limit). The computer has two
' levels, EASY (default: it acts 5 frames after each look) and NORMAL (2).
' The foot sweep is slow (16-frame wind-up) and needs to be close (12
' pixels, inside the punch's 14), so a punch beats it. After the same move
' three times running the computer expects it: a punch for a sweeper (it
' waits just outside the sweep's reach), a block for punches and kicks,
' answered 2 frames after its look even on EASY.
'
' Controls: joystick only. Player 1 (white) controller 1, player 2 (black)
' controller 2; in one-player mode white plays on either controller. On the
' title, up and down choose, a button starts (on COMPUTER, a button or
' left/right changes EASY / NORMAL).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.

